Subject: Pass the original object as the receiver in the borrowed field load

What this does
  Saves the receiver before the prototype walk in the fused local-field load and
  passes the saved value to the slow property path.

Why we need it
  The variable that holds the borrowed receiver is reassigned by the walk to the
  exotic object it stops on, and the slow path then handed that object on as the
  receiver.

What it adds
  The slow path reads the receiver back from the frame slot the load borrowed it
  from, instead of keeping a second local alive for the whole handler. A proxy's
  get trap and a getter on a prototype now observe the object the program named.
  The returned value, the borrowed arguments of the fallback, own properties and
  the missing-property case are unchanged. No opcode is added or retired and
  BC_VERSION is unchanged.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -22279,12 +22279,10 @@
                         }
                     } else {
                     get_loc_field_nr_slow:
-                        /* Fallback: the generic getter borrows obj (does not free
-                           it), exactly as the plain OP_get_field slow path relies on.
-                           The receiver is the borrowed local, so we pass it directly
-                           and never free it. */
                         sf->cur_pc = pc;
+                        /* owns neither argument; the receiver is the slot, not
+                           obj, which the prototype walk may have reassigned */
-                        val = JS_GetPropertyInternal(ctx, obj, atom, obj, false);
+                        val = JS_GetPropertyInternal(ctx, obj, atom, nr_buf[loc], false);
                         if (unlikely(JS_IsException(val)))
                             goto exception;
                     }
